{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/e9cvixcz","slide":1,"text":"What this identifies: whether a local AI error shock changes later AI adoption beyond what can be explained by average performance.","textPreview":"What this identifies: whether a local AI error shock changes later AI adoption beyond what can be explained by average performance.","textChars":131,"textLines":1,"bbox":[128,653,1016,18]}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/vopgpg","slide":1,"text":"This slide gives the concrete experimental procedure before moving into the identification table.\nParticipants complete a repeated abstract allocation task. In each round, they first see recent history for three abstract channels, make their own allocation, then see the AI allocation for the same round. They choose whether to submit their own plan or the AI plan.\nThe critical design feature is full feedback. After each round, participants can observe both the payoff of the chosen option and the counterfactual payoff of the unchosen option. This means they can continuously compare self judgment and AI judgment.\nAcross participants, the experiment manipulates the timing structure of AI errors. Some paths distribute errors across the window, while shock paths concentrate errors in a pre-registered block and then allow AI performance to recover. Overall AI performance, error count, and error severity are kept as comparable as possible, so later under-reliance cannot simply be explained by the AI being worse on average."}